INT. COURTHOUSE - WITNESS PREPARATION ROOM - DAY

A windowless room. A table, two chairs, a pitcher of water nobody has touched. A wall clock with a loud second hand.

ROSALIND ACHTERBERG, forties, a defense attorney in a charcoal suit, sits with a legal pad. Across from her, OSWALD TIMMONS, fifties, a quiet accountant in a borrowed tie, smooths his sleeves.

ROSALIND
We have nine minutes. Do you remember what I told you?

OSWALD
Answer the question asked. Don't volunteer.

ROSALIND
Good.

OSWALD
Look at the jury.

ROSALIND
Not too much.

OSWALD
Not too much.

She writes something. He drinks water, then sets the cup down.

OSWALD (CONT'D)
Ms. Achterberg. May I ask you something?

ROSALIND
Of course.

OSWALD
When this is over, will you still be my lawyer?

ROSALIND
Mr. Timmons, I'll be your lawyer until the court says I'm not.

OSWALD
That's not what I asked.

She looks at him a moment. The clock ticks.

ROSALIND
Yes.

He nods, relieved, and folds his hands.

OSWALD
It's strange. I've kept books for thirty years. Never lost a penny of anyone else's.

ROSALIND
I know. That's what the jury will hear.

OSWALD
They won't hear the ledger?

ROSALIND
They won't read the ledger. They'll hear you.

OSWALD
Then I should stop fidgeting.

ROSALIND
You're doing fine.

OSWALD
You're kind.

ROSALIND
I'm accurate.

He smiles faintly. She turns a page on the legal pad.

ROSALIND (CONT'D)
One more thing. Direct examination goes first, then cross. The prosecutor will try to rattle you with the Cayman accounts.

OSWALD
I understand.

ROSALIND
What do you say?

OSWALD
I say I reported them. Fully. On the forms the bank gave me.

ROSALIND
And if he asks about the second set of forms?

OSWALD
(a beat)
What second set?

ROSALIND
Exactly. Don't get cute.

OSWALD
I'm not cute.

ROSALIND
No. You're not.

The clock ticks. Rosalind caps her pen.

ROSALIND (CONT'D)
Oswald. Look at me.

OSWALD
I'm looking.

ROSALIND
Is there a second set of forms?

OSWALD
No.

ROSALIND
Think carefully.

OSWALD
There is no second set of forms.

ROSALIND
Because if there is, I need to know now, not on the stand. I can still protect you now.

OSWALD
Ms. Achterberg.

ROSALIND
Yes?

OSWALD
You've been wonderful.

Rosalind studies him. She does not speak. The clock ticks four more times.

ROSALIND
Mr. Timmons, why did you say "wonderful"?

OSWALD
Because you have.

ROSALIND
You said it like goodbye.

OSWALD
Did I?

ROSALIND
Yes.

A knock at the door. A BAILIFF's voice carries through.

BAILIFF (O.S.)
Two minutes, counselor.

ROSALIND
Thank you.

She doesn't move. Oswald stands, straightens his tie, and picks up a thin manila folder from the floor beside his chair. He sets it on the table in front of her.

OSWALD
I was going to give you this after.

She opens it. Inside, a second set of forms. She reads the first page, then the second, and her face remains still.

ROSALIND
When did you find these?

OSWALD
I didn't find them. I made them.

ROSALIND
Oswald.

OSWALD
I thought you should know before I swear to tell the truth.

ROSALIND
Why?

OSWALD
Because I would like very much to do it.

Rosalind closes the folder. The second hand ticks. Neither moves.

ROSALIND
Sit down.

He sits.

ROSALIND (CONT'D)
We're going to ask for a continuance. And you're going to tell me everything, from the beginning.

OSWALD
All right.

ROSALIND ^
Start with the first account.

OSWALD
(speaking over her)
Start with the first account.

He smiles. She almost does.
